usage.examples:
A standalone primary action:

```html
<ki-button variant="primary">Save</ki-button>
```

In momentary mode `type` defaults to `submit`. Use `type="button"` for an independent action inside a form.

```html
<form>
  <ki-button type="button">Cancel</ki-button>
  <ki-button variant="primary">Save changes</ki-button>
</form>
```

A call to action that navigates takes `href`: the button renders a native link with the same look. Enter follows it, it never submits its form, and `target="_blank"` without `rel` gets `rel="noreferrer noopener"`.

```html
<ki-button variant="primary" href="/start">Get started</ki-button>
```

Use `toggle` for a persistent action. `selected` sets the starting state and each activation changes `aria-pressed` while keeping the label. Toggle mode acts independently of form submission and reset, regardless of `type`.

```html
<ki-button id="pin" variant="outline" toggle selected>Pin</ki-button>
<script type="module">
  const button = document.getElementById('pin');
  button.addEventListener('ki-change', ({ detail: { selected } }) => {
    console.log('Pinned', selected);
  });
</script>
```

Enabled outline actions use `--ki-button-outline-hover-bg`,
`--ki-button-outline-hover-fg` and `--ki-button-outline-hover-border` on hover.
Unselected filled and quiet toggles use `--ki-button-toggle-unselected-hover-overlay`.
A selected toggle keeps its fill on hover. Use `--ki-button-outline-selected-border`
to set the selected outline edge.

Programmatic writes to `selected` are silent. A parent coordinating several toggles can listen for their bubbling `ki-change` events and update their `selected` values together.

Open a dialog with `command="show-modal"` and its ID in `commandfor`. The
dialog and invoker share a document or shadow root. The command takes
precedence over form submission or reset.

```html
<ki-button commandfor="draft-preview" command="show-modal">Preview draft</ki-button>
<ki-dialog id="draft-preview" heading="Draft preview">
  This draft is visible only to you.
  <ki-button slot="footer" commandfor="draft-preview" command="close">Done</ki-button>
</ki-dialog>
```

Programmatic creation after registration; string attributes and properties are interchangeable:

```ts
import { defineCustomElement } from '@kimen/elements/ki-button';

defineCustomElement();

const saveButton = document.createElement('ki-button');
saveButton.setAttribute('type', 'button');
saveButton.textContent = 'Save';
document.body.append(saveButton);
```

The leading and trailing slots use `--ki-button-{size}-icon-size` for their
box and for a directly slotted `ki-icon` glyph. The button token takes
precedence over an explicit `size` on that icon.

While the action a button started is in progress, set `loading` on it instead of `disabled`: it keeps its width and focus, ignores further activation (it neither submits nor resets its form), draws a spinner in place of its label and announces `loading-label` (default "Loading") as a polite status message. Clear it when the action ends:

```html
<form id="profile">
  <ki-button variant="primary" loading-label="Saving">Save changes</ki-button>
</form>
<script type="module">
  const form = document.getElementById('profile');
  const save = form.querySelector('ki-button');
  form.addEventListener('submit', async (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();
    save.loading = true;
    try {
      await fetch('/profile', { method: 'POST', body: new FormData(form) });
    } finally {
      save.loading = false;
    }
  });
</script>
```

A button or link fits its content by default. Set `--ki-button-inline-size`
to `100%` to fill the containing block. The alignment tokens start-align the
content and label lines of this two-line choice.

```html
<style>
  .choice {
    --ki-button-inline-size: 100%;
    --ki-button-justify-content: start;
    --ki-button-text-align: start;
  }
</style>
<ki-button class="choice" variant="outline" toggle>
  <strong>Starter</strong><br />One agent, one repository
</ki-button>
```

# Carousel examples

```html
<ki-carousel label="Product tour" slides-per-view="2" slides-per-move="2">
  <ki-carousel-item>Discover the tools</ki-carousel-item>
  <ki-carousel-item>Pick a theme</ki-carousel-item>
  <ki-carousel-item>Set up your workspace</ki-carousel-item>
  <ki-carousel-item>Share your first surface</ki-carousel-item>
</ki-carousel>
```

```js
const carousel = document.querySelector('ki-carousel');
carousel.addEventListener('ki-slide-change', ({ detail }) => {
  console.log(detail.index);
});
await carousel.goTo(3);
```

With per-tag imports, define both `ki-carousel` and `ki-carousel-item`.

# Confirm an assistant action

```html
<ki-confirmation
  id="send-email-prompt"
  heading="Send this email?"
  action="send_email"
  approve-label="Send email"
  deny-label="Keep draft"
  consequence="Recipients will get the message immediately."
  remember
  remember-label="Allow sending email for the rest of this conversation"
>
  <dl>
    <dt>To</dt><dd>Ana Rivera</dd>
    <dt>Subject</dt><dd>Weekly report</dd>
  </dl>
</ki-confirmation>
```

A guarded UI spec binds each decision to its own action, one per event. A
binding by name alone is rejected, because the component declares two
events. `props.action` carries the visible tool identifier as text, never as
a URL, so the card renders under an origin allowlist too.

```json
{
  "version": 1,
  "actions": ["approve-send-email", "deny-send-email"],
  "root": {
    "component": "ki-confirmation",
    "props": {
      "heading": "Send this email?",
      "action": "send_email",
      "approve-label": "Send email",
      "deny-label": "Keep draft",
      "consequence": "Recipients will get the message immediately."
    },
    "action": { "ki-approve": "approve-send-email", "ki-deny": "deny-send-email" }
  }
}
```

The action data carries the card's props, not the event's detail. When the
decision needs the conversation preference, map the two events in trusted
host code instead. This MCP Apps example uses a ready `SurfaceBridge` whose
rendered spec declares `approve-send-email` and `deny-send-email` in its
top-level `actions` and leaves the card unbound.

```js
const prompt = document.querySelector('#send-email-prompt');

prompt.addEventListener('ki-approve', (event) => {
  if (event.composedPath()[0] !== prompt) return;
  bridge.dispatch('approve-send-email', {
    tool: 'send_email',
    remember: event.detail?.remember === true,
  });
});

prompt.addEventListener('ki-deny', (event) => {
  if (event.composedPath()[0] !== prompt) return;
  bridge.dispatch('deny-send-email', { tool: 'send_email' });
});
```

The host owns execution, deadlines and any conversation permission. Supply
outcome metadata and set the resolved state after recording the decision.

```js
prompt.resolvedBy = 'You';
prompt.resolvedAt = '10:32';
prompt.state = 'approved';
```

Call `await prompt.focusPrompt()` when the person is waiting for the request.
Keep the proposed inputs stable while the person is deciding. Mount a new
element if another request for the same tool replaces a pending one.

# ki-conversation examples

## Bounded transcript

Set a block size to make the transcript scroll. Enable `stick-to-bottom` to
follow new messages and keep incoming replies in view.

```html
<ki-conversation id="help-chat" label="Component help" stick-to-bottom style="block-size: 20rem">
  <ki-message message-role="user">Which inputs handle form errors?</ki-message>
  <ki-message message-role="event" icon-name="search">Searched the component docs</ki-message>
  <ki-message message-role="assistant">Input, textarea and select show field errors.</ki-message>
</ki-conversation>
<label for="prompt">Message</label>
<textarea id="prompt"></textarea>
```

## Pass application chunks to a message

Call this function with the async iterable supplied by your application.
Keep the composer and any waiting indicator outside the live log.

```js
async function appendReply(conversation, chunks) {
  const reply = document.createElement('ki-message');
  reply.setAttribute('streaming', '');
  conversation.append(reply);
  try {
    for await (const chunk of chunks) {
      reply.append(chunk);
    }
  } finally {
    reply.removeAttribute('streaming');
  }
}
```

## Custom-elements entry points

```ts
import { defineCustomElement as defineConversation } from '@kimen/elements/ki-conversation';
import { defineCustomElement as defineMessage } from '@kimen/elements/ki-message';

defineConversation();
defineMessage();
```

## Edit details beside a list

Use `heading` to name the sheet. Open it from a focused trigger so focus can
return there. The app wires its own footer actions.

```html
<ki-button id="edit-incident" type="button">Edit incident</ki-button>
<ki-drawer id="incident-editor" heading="Edit incident" placement="end">
  <ki-input label="Title" value="Search index rebuild"></ki-input>
  <ki-button slot="footer" id="save-incident" type="button" variant="primary">
    Save
  </ki-button>
</ki-drawer>

<script type="module">
  const editor = document.querySelector('#incident-editor');
  document.querySelector('#edit-incident').addEventListener('click', () => editor.show());
  document.querySelector('#save-incident').addEventListener('click', () => {
    // Save the app's changes first.
    editor.close();
  });
  editor.addEventListener('ki-close', (event) => {
    if (editor.hasAttribute('data-unsaved')) event.preventDefault();
  });
</script>
```

## Offer a short list of actions

A bottom sheet has a swipe handle and a keyboard-accessible Close button.
Use `close-label` to translate the button for your audience.

```html
<ki-drawer heading="Share incident" placement="bottom" size="20rem" open>
  <p>Choose how to share the incident.</p>
  <ki-button slot="footer" variant="secondary">Email</ki-button>
  <ki-button slot="footer" variant="primary">Copy link</ki-button>
</ki-drawer>
```

Use `modal="false"` when the page behind the drawer should stay interactive.
`placement="start"` and `placement="end"` follow the inherited direction.

# Using ki-feature-icon

Load the token core and the sheets of the tags you write, register each
custom element, and import the Lucide glyph you use. Place one glyph with its default inherited size
in the slot, next to the heading that names the section.

```js
import '@kimen/tokens/css/core';
import '@kimen/tokens/css/ki-feature-icon';
import '@kimen/tokens/css/ki-icon';
import '@kimen/elements/icons/shield-check';
import { defineCustomElement as defineFeatureIcon } from '@kimen/elements/ki-feature-icon';
import { defineCustomElement as defineIcon } from '@kimen/elements/ki-icon';

defineFeatureIcon();
defineIcon();
```

```html
<div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:var(--ki-space-md)">
  <ki-feature-icon tone="primary-light" size="md">
    <ki-icon name="shield-check"></ki-icon>
  </ki-feature-icon>
  <h2>Security settings</h2>
</div>
```

Choose a circle with `shape="circle"`. For an inline SVG, use `currentColor`
for the glyph's stroke or fill. Keep controls and meaningful labels outside
the tile so they can receive focus and be announced.

A guarded catalog surface supplies the glyph as a `ki-icon` node in the
default slot. The host imports the glyph before rendering the surface.

```json
{
  "version": 1,
  "root": {
    "component": "div",
    "props": { "direction": "row", "align": "center", "gap": "md" },
    "slots": {
      "": [
        {
          "component": "ki-feature-icon",
          "props": { "tone": "primary-light" },
          "slots": {
            "": [{ "component": "ki-icon", "props": { "name": "shield-check" } }]
          }
        },
        { "component": "h2", "slots": { "": ["Security settings"] } }
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

# File upload examples

## Submit attachments with a form

```html
<form action="/attachments" method="post" enctype="multipart/form-data">
  <ki-file-upload
    label="Incident attachments"
    name="attachments"
    accept=".csv,.pdf"
    max-size="10MB"
    max-files="3"
    multiple
    required
  >
    <span slot="help">Choose up to three CSV or PDF files, up to 10 MB each.</span>
  </ki-file-upload>
  <button type="submit">Send attachments</button>
  <button type="reset">Clear attachments</button>
</form>
```

Register the element once with the direct package entry point.

```js
import { defineCustomElement } from '@kimen/elements/ki-file-upload';

defineCustomElement();
```

The form submits each accepted original `File` under `attachments`. A reset
clears the list. Use `error-text` for a form error, such as one returned by
your application, and clear it when the error is resolved.

## Report progress from an application-managed transfer

Use a standalone picker when your application sends files as soon as people
choose them.

```html
<ki-file-upload id="transfer-picker" label="Import CSV files" accept=".csv" multiple>
</ki-file-upload>
```

Here `sendFile` is your application's transfer function. It accepts a
`File`, an abort signal and a progress callback receiving a percentage.

```js
const upload = document.querySelector('#transfer-picker');
const transfers = new Map();

function update(file, changes) {
  upload.files = upload.files.map((entry) =>
    entry.file === file ? { ...entry, ...changes } : entry,
  );
}

async function startUpload(file) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  transfers.set(file, controller);
  update(file, { status: 'uploading', progress: 0 });

  try {
    await sendFile(file, {
      signal: controller.signal,
      onProgress: (progress) => update(file, { progress }),
    });
    update(file, { status: 'done' });
  } catch {
    const error = controller.signal.aborted ? 'Upload canceled.' : 'Upload failed.';
    update(file, { status: 'error', error });
  } finally {
    transfers.delete(file);
  }
}

upload.addEventListener('ki-files-add', ({ detail }) => {
  for (const { file } of detail.files) {
    void startUpload(file);
  }
});

upload.addEventListener('ki-file-cancel', ({ detail }) => {
  transfers.get(detail.file.file)?.abort();
});
```

Use `ki-file-remove` to update application state when a row is removed.
For previously uploaded attachments, set `files` to rows with metadata and
`status: 'done'`. Only rows carrying an original `File` contribute form
entries.

# ki-icon examples

Import the exact Lucide names used by your markup before you mount it.

```js
import '@kimen/elements/icons/circle-check';
import '@kimen/elements/icons/triangle-alert';
import { defineCustomElement } from '@kimen/elements/ki-icon';

defineCustomElement();
```

A confirmation has adjacent text, so the glyph is decorative.

```html
<span style="color: var(--ki-text-success-high-em)">
  <ki-icon name="circle-check"></ki-icon> Confirmed
</span>
```

A standalone meaningful glyph takes its accessible name from the label.

```html
<ki-icon name="triangle-alert" size="lg" label="Warning"></ki-icon>
```

For a guarded catalog surface, import the glyph in the host and reference it
by name. The icon has no slots or actions.

```json
{
  "version": 1,
  "root": {
    "component": "ki-icon",
    "props": { "name": "triangle-alert", "size": "lg", "label": "Warning" }
  }
}
```

# ki-markdown examples

The supported Markdown subset includes headings, paragraphs, emphasis,
strikethrough, inline and fenced code, links, opt-in images, ordered and
unordered lists, task items, blockquotes, tables and hard breaks.
Use `> [!NOTE]` to display a Kimen Note.

The loader defines Markdown and its Link, CodeBlock and Note children as they appear. Register the
child elements your content uses when using direct imports or framework
wrappers.

```js
import { defineCustomElement as defineCodeBlock } from '@kimen/elements/ki-code-block';
import { defineCustomElement as defineLink } from '@kimen/elements/ki-link';
import { defineCustomElement as defineNote } from '@kimen/elements/ki-note';
import { defineCustomElement as defineMarkdown } from '@kimen/elements/ki-markdown';

defineCodeBlock();
defineLink();
defineNote();
defineMarkdown();
```

With a framework wrapper, use the first three registrations. The wrapper
registers Markdown.

Render a short note as static text.

```html
<ki-markdown src-text="## Release notes"></ki-markdown>

<ki-markdown>Check the **release date** and the [upgrade guide](/guides/upgrade).</ki-markdown>
```

Update the source while chunks arrive. When all text has arrived, finish the
stream so the component renders the complete Markdown document.

```html
<ki-markdown id="answer" streaming></ki-markdown>
```

```js
const answer = document.querySelector('#answer');
let source = '';
for await (const chunk of answerChunks) {
  source += chunk;
  answer.srcText = source;
}
answer.streaming = false;
```

Assign untrusted strings to `srcText`. In a static HTML example, escape angle
brackets before passing them as text.

```html
<ki-markdown>&lt;aside&gt;This markup is shown as text.&lt;/aside&gt;</ki-markdown>
```

A guarded surface can pass the source as a string in the default slot.

```json
{
  "version": 1,
  "root": {
    "component": "ki-markdown",
    "props": { "heading-offset": 1 },
    "slots": { "": ["# Release notes\n\nRead the [upgrade guide](/guides/upgrade)."] }
  }
}
```

# Pagination examples

Import `@kimen/tokens/css/core` and `@kimen/tokens/css/ki-pagination`, and
register the element with `defineCustomElement` from
`@kimen/elements/components/ki-pagination.js`.

## Replace a results page

```html
<h2 id="results-heading" tabindex="-1">Results page 1</h2>
<ul id="results"><li>Mercury</li><li>Venus</li></ul>
<ki-pagination id="results-pages" page="1" pages="2" label="Planet results pages"></ki-pagination>

<script type="module">
  const pages = [['Mercury', 'Venus'], ['Earth', 'Mars']];
  const pager = document.getElementById('results-pages');
  const heading = document.getElementById('results-heading');
  const results = document.getElementById('results');

  pager.addEventListener('ki-page-change', ({ detail: { page } }) => {
    const items = pages[page - 1].map((name) => {
      const item = document.createElement('li');
      item.textContent = name;
      return item;
    });
    results.replaceChildren(...items);
    heading.textContent = `Results page ${page}`;
    pager.page = page;
    heading.focus();
  });
</script>
```

When fetching asynchronously, update `page` and move focus after the
requested results render. Keep the heading programmatically focusable.

## Let the server render each page

```html
<ki-pagination page="5" pages="12" href-template="?page={page}"
  label="Search results pages"></ki-pagination>
```

Place `{page}` in the path, query, or fragment of a relative or HTTP(S) URL.
The generated anchors support browser link actions. The catalog applies
its URL policy. For a guarded page action, use numbered or compact pagination
in button mode.

## Append results and announce the count

```html
<ul id="recent-results"><li>Mercury</li><li>Venus</li></ul>
<ki-pagination id="recent-pagination" variant="load-more" shown="2" total="4"
  label="More planets"></ki-pagination>

<script type="module">
  const pager = document.getElementById('recent-pagination');
  const results = document.getElementById('recent-results');

  pager.addEventListener('ki-load-more', () => {
    const items = ['Earth', 'Mars'].map((name) => {
      const item = document.createElement('li');
      item.textContent = name;
      return item;
    });
    results.append(...items);
    pager.shown = results.childElementCount;
  });
</script>
```

The last update keeps the same button in place and announces the final
count. Set `previous-label`, `next-label`, and `load-more-label` for
translated controls. Use `page-label`, `compact-label`, and `status-label`
with the named placeholders to translate the generated text.

props.docs:
Completed amount. Presentation and ARIA clamp this value to `0..max`;
malformed values fall back to `0`. Ignored while `indeterminate` is set.
When to use: set with `max` for determinate task advancement.
When NOT to use: do not set a fabricated value for unknown-duration work;
set `indeterminate` instead.

